(defmodule sandbox-events
  "**The router between the editor's evaluation sessions and everything that listens to
them** — four sessions now, and one set of folds for all of them.

Every backend is consumer-blind: it knows how to spawn a child and put a request on its
wire, and nothing about a model. What it emits is `session`'s three records — `ready`,
`reply`, `down` — each carrying the session's own NAME, and the three `UiEvent` impls here
fan each one out to the clients of THAT session in [[*clients*]].

Three things that shape this file:

**The impls cannot live with a client.** An `impl` is per record type, so while they sat in
`tutor` a second client could not add one, and the tutorial routed replies for features it
knows nothing about.

**Nor can they live per backend.** Each session used to carry its own three records purely
so these folds could tell whose event they were holding — a fact the session already knows
about itself. Once the name rode on the event, six impls became three and three backends
stopped carrying an event vocabulary at all.

**Fanning out beats addressing.** A reply carries an `:id`, ids are minted from one counter
for every session, and every client already drops an id it did not send — so \"not mine\" is
a no-op on every path and the wire needs no owner tag.

The one event that needs a decision rather than a fan-out is `down` with nothing watching:
the sandbox used to be shut down when the *Tutorial* buffer was gone, which with two clients
would have killed it out from under an open playground. That is a question about ALL of that
session's clients — [[any-client-live?]] — which is exactly what no single client can
answer about itself."
  (:use model) ; the model the folds thread
  (:use session)) ; the three event records + the shared shutdown
;; Each client, as the symbols naming its four hooks. Symbols, resolved at fold time:
;; `tutor` and `playground` are both DEFERRED modules (loaded on first use), so a
;; registration call at load time would either force them eager or arrive too late. A
;; `bound?` guard on the symbol is the same late-binding discipline keymaps use for a
;; command, and it means an unloaded client simply isn't a client yet.
;;
;; Adding a client is one entry here. Its hooks:
;;   :session                    -> which session's events it wants (`:sandbox`, `:elixir-test`, …)
;;   :live?  (m)                 -> is this client's buffer on screen? (does it still want a session)
;;   :ready  (m gen banner)      -> the child announced itself; the session is empty again
;;   :reply  (m reply gen)       -> one decoded reply; MUST ignore an id it didn't send
;;   :down   (m code)            -> the child went away (a respawn usually follows)
;;
;; The BANNER on `:ready` is the child's own first word about itself. A Brood sandbox's says
;; only that it is up; the Elixir agent's says which application started and which Elixir is
;; running, which nothing on this side could work out. Clients that do not care ignore it.
;;
;; `:session` arrived with the second one. A reply is safe to fan out blindly — ids are
;; minted from one counter for every session and a client drops an id it did not send — but
;; `ready` and `down` are facts about a PARTICULAR child, and fanning those out blindly told
;; the Brood playground its sandbox had died whenever the Elixir agent did. Worse, it
;; answered "is anyone still watching?" with a yes from a client of the other session, which
;; would have kept a dead Elixir agent respawning behind an open Brood playground — and in a
;; mix project each of those respawns recompiles the project.
(def *clients*
  [{:session :sandbox
    :live? 'tutor/tutor-live-buffer?
    :ready 'tutor/tutor-sandbox-ready
    :reply 'tutor/tutor-sandbox-reply
    :down 'tutor/tutor-sandbox-down}
    {:session :sandbox
     :live? 'playground/playground-live-buffer?
     :ready 'playground/playground-sandbox-ready
     :reply 'playground/playground-sandbox-reply
     :down 'playground/playground-sandbox-down}
    ;; The Elixir playground is a client of a DIFFERENT session, whose child is a BEAM node
    ;; rather than a Brood runtime. Same list, same hooks, same discipline.
    {:session :elixir-sandbox
     :live? 'elixir-playground/elixir-playground-live-buffer?
     :ready 'elixir-playground/elixir-playground-ready
     :reply 'elixir-playground/elixir-playground-reply
     :down 'elixir-playground/elixir-playground-down}
    ;; *BEAM Processes* shares the playground's agent rather than starting a second one:
    ;; the point of observing from inside the node is that it is the node your application
    ;; is running in, and a second child would be a second VM with nothing in it.
    {:session :elixir-sandbox
     :live? 'beam/beam-live-buffer?
     :ready 'beam/beam-ready
     :reply 'beam/beam-reply
     :down 'beam/beam-down}
    ;; the model itself: a brood buffer's diagnostics are `:check` requests to the child
    ;; rooted in the buffer's project (`model/ed-launch-diag`)
    {:session :sandbox
     :live? 'model/ed-diag-client-live?
     :ready 'model/ed-diag-sandbox-ready
     :reply 'model/ed-diag-sandbox-reply
     :down 'model/ed-diag-sandbox-down}
    ;; …and the warm TEST node, which is a third session: its own VM, in its own
    ;; environment, so a `down` from it says nothing about the playground's. `testrun`
    ;; folded its events with impls of its own until the name started riding on them; now
    ;; it is a row here like everything else.
    {:session :elixir-test
     :live? 'testrun/warm-live-elixir?
     :ready 'testrun/warm-ready-elixir
     :reply 'testrun/warm-reply
     :down 'testrun/warm-down-elixir}
    ;; …and the Brood one, which is the same story in the language the editor is written
    ;; in: a `nest test` of one file spends most of its second loading the project, not
    ;; running tests, and a session pays that once.
    {:session :brood-test
     :live? 'testrun/warm-live-brood?
     :ready 'testrun/warm-ready-brood
     :reply 'testrun/warm-reply
     :down 'testrun/warm-down-brood}])

(defn clients-of (session)
  "The registered clients of `session` — what every fold below is scoped to."
  (seq/filter *clients* (fn (client) (= (get client :session) session))))

(defn ensure ()
  "Force this module to load, registering the three `UiEvent` impls with it. Call it wherever
a client starts the sandbox.

An `impl` is registered by LOADING the file that carries it, and every client here is a
deferred module — so nothing would have loaded the router until an event arrived, which is
exactly too late. A qualified call is the load; the nil is incidental."
  nil)

(defn- call-hook (sym args fallback)
  ;; Call a registered hook if its module is loaded, else answer `fallback`. Never lets one
  ;; client's fold take out the loop: a raise here would arrive as a dead editor on an event
  ;; the user did not cause, so it degrades to "that client saw nothing" instead.
  (if (not (bound? sym))
    fallback
    (try (apply (reflect/eval sym) args) (catch _ fallback))))

(defn fan-out (session m hook args)
  "Thread `m` through every loaded client of `session` for `hook` (`:ready` / `:reply` /
`:down`), each called with `m` followed by `args`. A client that isn't loaded, or whose
fold raises, is skipped with the model untouched."
  (fold (clients-of session)
    m
    (fn (acc client) (call-hook (get client hook) (cons acc args) acc))))

(defn any-client-live? (session m)
  "True while at least one client of `session` still has its buffer on screen — i.e.
somebody still wants that child. What decides whether a dead one is restarted or let go:
with a single client it was \"is the tutorial open?\", and it can only be asked of all of
that session's clients — and only of THAT session's, or an open Brood playground would keep
a dead Elixir agent respawning forever."
  (any? (clients-of session) (fn (client) (call-hook (get client :live?) (list m) false))))

(impl UiEvent
  session/session-ready
  ;; The BANNER travels alongside the generation, always. A Brood sandbox's says only that
  ;; it is up; the Elixir agent's says which application started and which Elixir is
  ;; running — facts only the child can know. A client that does not care ignores the
  ;; argument; the ones that do could not be told any other way.
  (ui-event-fold [e m textrows cols rows]
    (fan-out (session/session-ready-session e)
      m
      :ready
      (list (session/session-ready-gen e) (session/session-ready-banner e)))))

(impl UiEvent
  session/session-reply
  ;; The generation travels to each client rather than being checked here: a superseded
  ;; child's straggler is only droppable against the generation the CLIENT last saw, and
  ;; two clients can be a reset apart.
  (ui-event-fold [e m textrows cols rows]
    (fan-out (session/session-reply-session e)
      m
      :reply
      (list (session/session-reply-reply e) (session/session-reply-gen e)))))

(impl UiEvent
  session/session-down
  ;; The child (or the whole worker) went away. If nobody is watching that session any
  ;; more, stop it for good — a respawn must not outlive every UI that wanted it, and for
  ;; the Elixir sessions it is not merely a spare process: respawning one in a mix project
  ;; recompiles the project. Otherwise each client says its own piece about the restart.
  (ui-event-fold [e m textrows cols rows]
    (let (name (session/session-down-session e)
          code (session/session-down-code e)
          ;; Every client hears it either way. A `:down` hook is not a notification that
          ;; your buffer is open — it is the fact that the child holding your state is
          ;; gone, and a client with its buffer closed is exactly the one that would
          ;; otherwise keep believing a dead session was ready (or still attached to
          ;; somebody's node).
          m1 (fan-out name m :down (list code)))
      (if (any-client-live? name m1)
        m1
        ;; …and with nobody watching, stop it for good: a respawn must not outlive every
        ;; UI that wanted it, and for the Elixir sessions it is not merely a spare
        ;; process — respawning one in a mix project recompiles the project.
        (do (session/shutdown name) m1)))))
